AWS SNS — 

Amazon Simple Notification Service (SNS) is a fully managed messaging and notification service from AWS.

Its main purpose is to send messages/notifications from one publisher to multiple subscribers.

1. Simple Meaning

Think of SNS like a notification broadcast system.

              SNS Topic
             /    |    \
            ↓     ↓     ↓
          Email   SMS   SQS

One message can be delivered to multiple destinations.

2. Why do we need SNS?

Suppose an application detects that an EC2 server is unhealthy.

You may want to notify:

DevOps team through email
An application through SQS
Someone through SMS
Another AWS service

Instead of sending each notification separately, the application can publish one message to an SNS topic.

SNS distributes that message to the configured subscribers.

3. Important SNS Components
Topic

A Topic is a communication channel.

Example:

EC2-Alerts

Publishers send messages to the topic.

Subscribers receive messages from the topic.

Publisher

The publisher is the component that sends a message to SNS.

Examples:

Application
EC2
Lambda
CloudWatch
Other AWS services

Example:

CloudWatch → SNS Topic
Subscriber

A subscriber is the destination that receives messages from SNS.

Examples:

Email
SMS
SQS
Lambda
HTTP/HTTPS endpoint
4. How SNS Works

Basic flow:

Publisher
    ↓
SNS Topic
    ↓
 ┌──┴─────┬──────┬──────┐
 ↓        ↓      ↓      ↓
Email    SQS   Lambda   SMS

Example:

CloudWatch detects an alarm.
CloudWatch publishes a message to SNS.
SNS receives the message.
SNS sends the message to subscribed endpoints.
Subscribers process the notification.
5. SNS Pub/Sub Model

SNS uses a Publish/Subscribe (Pub/Sub) model.

Publish

A publisher sends a message to a topic.

Subscribe

Consumers subscribe to the topic.

Notify

SNS distributes published messages to subscribers.

Publisher
    ↓
 Publish
    ↓
  Topic
    ↓
 Notify subscribers
6. SNS Topic Types

There are two important SNS topic types:

Standard Topic

Designed for high-throughput messaging.

Characteristics:

Very high throughput
At-least-once message delivery
Messages can potentially be delivered more than once
Message ordering isn't guaranteed
FIFO Topic

FIFO = First In, First Out

Designed when message ordering and deduplication are important.

Characteristics:

Message ordering
Message deduplication
More controlled message processing
